HCS/0.2
@title HCS - Holographic Context Standard (reference CLI)
@root .
@updated 2026-10-21T23:17:58.001Z
@meta license=MIT language=TypeScript

[claims]

## Overview
#purpose[manual] HCS is a plain-text format plus a zero-dependency Node.js CLI and MCP server (`hcs`) that makes LLM context traceable (claims cite sources), verifiable (anchors carry hashes) and measured (probes and fidelity certificates).
  > README.md:3-16 #cddd3cef50fb
#layers[manual] Deterministic commands (init, validate, check, seal, render, stats, audit, serve, impact, keygen, badge) never call an LLM; compile, score, curve, compare and snapshot need a `provider:model` LLM; models only lists a provider's model ids.
  > src/cli.ts::HELP #b0da33a71ed9

## Format
#magic[manual] A file starts with `HCS/0.3` (0.1 and 0.2 are still accepted, and the writer emits 0.2 unless a 0.3 feature is used: probe tags, dependency hashes or the `@parser` header), then `@key value` headers, then optional [claims], [probes] and [certificates] blocks; errors carry line numbers.
  > src/format.ts::parse #3b7f2aa6aa3c
  > src/format.ts::SUPPORTED_VERSIONS #bfa61b8b8d04
#anchor-syntax[manual] Anchors are `> path`, `> path:start-end` or `> path::Symbol`, optionally followed by `#hash` (`#hash/deps` for symbol anchors with a dependency hash); quoted forms allow spaces in paths and symbols.
  > src/format.ts::parseAnchor #77403b0d552c
  > src/format.ts::formatAnchor #744130728627

## Verification
#hash[manual] Hashes are the first 12 hex chars of SHA-256 over LF-normalized text without trailing whitespace; symbol anchors also remove all whitespace, so reformatting does not break them.
  > src/anchors.ts::HASH_LENGTH #0330f2780307
  > src/anchors.ts::normalizeLines #1f9f0385cd31
  > src/anchors.ts::hashSymbolLines #c874fdcbc24f
#statuses[manual] checkAnchor returns ok, moved (same text elsewhere in the file, or the same symbol in another file), review (symbol unchanged, a dependency changed), stale, missing or unsealed; an item takes the worst status of its anchors.
  > src/anchors.ts::checkAnchor #860ca86a7421
  > src/anchors.ts::checkSymbolAnchor #ba6eb19e6540
  > src/anchors.ts::findMovedSymbol #e33d34d73e62
#symbols[manual] Symbols are located without dependencies for brace languages, Python, Markdown headings, YAML and JSON, ignoring strings, regex literals and comments; unresolvable symbols are reported missing, never guessed.
  > src/symbols.ts::listSymbols #9ff7204d2e67
  > src/symbols.ts::codeOnly #9e8476116018
#treesitter[manual] `--parser tree-sitter` (or HCS_PARSER) locates TypeScript, JavaScript and Python symbols with web-tree-sitter, loaded at run time only and qualified by the syntax tree; other languages keep the heuristic, sealing records `@parser tree-sitter`, and the CLI turns the back-end on for files that carry it or stops with an install hint.
  > src/treesitter.ts::enableTreeSitter #efbeaf0fb1c3
  > src/treesitter.ts::treeSymbols #cc8f83cade7e
  > src/cli.ts::chooseParser #b6191c8ad303
  > src/anchors.ts::recordParser #b241c9f1f675
#deps[manual] With `seal --deps`, JS/TS and Python symbol anchors also store a hash of the symbols they use directly (same-file symbols and class siblings found by identifier matching, plus names imported from project files), and check reports `review`, a non-failing status, when that hash changes while the symbol itself does not.
  > src/deps.ts::dependencies #6268650a4566
  > src/deps.ts::depsHash #2527f7f95d4c
  > src/anchors.ts::checkSymbolAnchor #ba6eb19e6540
#seal[manual] `hcs seal` hashes unsealed anchors and follows moved ones (new lines, file or symbol); `--rehash` accepts stale content and drops certificates; `--rehash` also accepts `review` anchors, and `--deps` adds dependency hashes.
  > src/anchors.ts::seal #15fc15a95ec5

## Conformance
#rfc[manual] Changes to the format (syntax, hashing, verification, certificate meaning) need an RFC in rfcs/ open for comments at least 7 days before code is merged; until SPEC 1.0, a minor version may add features but must not change the meaning or hash of anything older files contain.
  > rfcs/README.md::Process #d221ed5362e8
  > rfcs/README.md::"Versioning policy (current, pre-1.0)" #32487af6a0b5
#conformance[manual] conformance/ holds language-agnostic cases (case.json, input.hcs, src/, expected.json) for parse, serialize, symbols, find, hash, deps, check, seal, render, digest and verify (signatures); the TypeScript reference runs them with `npm run conformance`, on a copy outside any git repository.
  > conformance/reference.mjs::runCase #b53ebb38bd87
  > conformance/reference.mjs::withCopy #74a6c5ab0693
#python-impl[manual] python/ is a dependency-free Python (>= 3.9) implementation of parse, serialize, hashing, symbol location, dependency hashes, check, seal, render and signature verification (pure-Python Ed25519) that passes the same cases, and cross_check.py compares it with the reference on this repository's files and on mutated .hcs inputs.
  > python/tests/test_conformance.py::run_case #f67aa0d0e85f
  > conformance/cross_check.py::main #0af4ce68132d

## Compilation
#quote-guard[manual] The compiler discards any claim or probe whose quote is not found verbatim (ignoring whitespace, case and Markdown markup such as backticks, emphasis and link syntax) in the cited chunk, then anchors it to the smallest enclosing symbol under 80 lines, or to the quoted lines.
  > src/compile.ts::anchorFor #5e3194b4874f
  > src/sources.ts::locateQuote #d6a37b2c93e6
#incremental[manual] `compile --update` keeps items whose anchors are ok or moved, re-reads only dirty or uncovered files, never drops manual claims, regenerates overview claims and clears certificates.
  > src/compile.ts::compile #d68054f83887
#resume[manual] When the quota runs out, the provider fails, or a reply has no usable JSON, compile keeps what it extracted and lists the missing chunks in the `@incomplete` header; `compile --update` then compiles only those chunks.
  > src/compile.ts::compile #d68054f83887
#reasoning[manual] Reasoning models that spend the whole output budget before answering are retried once with a 16384-token budget, replies cut by the limit are counted, and JSON replies with unescaped quotes or backslashes are repaired before parsing.
  > src/llm.ts::REASONING_BUDGET #d98e35d80f53
  > src/llm.ts::repairJSON #14b284eb41cd
#score-resume[manual] `score` saves each probe result in .hcs-cache/ under a key built from the context digest, probes, models and settings; after a quota stop the same command resumes, and no certificate is written until every probe is scored.
  > src/cli.ts::scoreCacheFile #aa584d270023
  > src/score.ts::ScoreCache #642a022805fd
#debug-log[manual] With HCS_DEBUG_LOG set, every model request and reply is appended to that file as one JSON line, without headers or keys.
  > src/llm.ts::withDebugLog #3b77fdca4cf6
#probe-spread[manual] Probes are picked round-robin across chunks, independently of which claims were kept, so that scores measure the source rather than the summary.
  > src/compile.ts::spreadProbes #fe14d197cc5a

## Scoring
#score[manual] Each probe is answered from the rendered context only: UNKNOWN is an abstention, a reply containing the expected answer is correct without a judge, otherwise a judge grades correct, partial or incorrect (1, 0.5, 0), optionally over several runs.
  > src/score.ts::scoreContext #9b71d862413b
  > src/score.ts::POINTS #f5b535d01ef1
#rigor[manual] Scores report a reproducible 95% bootstrap confidence interval, a no-context baseline, an informative fidelity on probes unanswerable without context, and a warning when the model judged itself.
  > src/score.ts::bootstrapCI #09a88d67acf9
  > src/score.ts::scoreDocument #3429f04c4e76
#curve[manual] `hcs curve` scores the same probes at several token budgets, scoring once the budgets that render the same context, and reports the knee: the smallest budget whose fidelity reaches the lower bound of the best point's 95% CI; each point is a certificate tagged with the curve id and the knee, and a curve whose smallest budget is already within that interval is reported as flat.
  > src/score.ts::scoreCurve #ffc5613318bc
  > src/score.ts::kneeOf #cca6ea2cbcd4
#signatures[manual] Certificates can be signed with Ed25519 (node:crypto) over a canonical payload of their sorted fields; check reports each signature as trusted (key from --trusted or HCS_TRUSTED_KEYS), untrusted (key only declared in the file's @keys header), invalid or unknown-key, and fails when a signed certificate was edited after signing.
  > src/sign.ts::certificatePayload #d93d25315be9
  > src/sign.ts::checkSignature #38b06ee80645
#badge[manual] `hcs badge` draws an SVG badge only from a certificate that is current and signed by a trusted key, and refuses to run without trusted keys.
  > src/badge.ts::badgeSvg #cdf1bf5d9a51
#judges[manual] `score` accepts two judges: each judged answer is graded by both, the stricter verdict counts, and the raw agreement and Cohen's kappa are reported in the certificate.
  > src/score.ts::scoreContext #9b71d862413b
  > src/score.ts::cohenKappa #2be6be699209
#known[manual] `compile --filter-known` asks every probe without context to the listed models and tags `[known]` the probes all of them answer correctly; `score` then reports a novel fidelity on the other probes.
  > src/score.ts::findKnownProbes #9b9804fedfc9
#compare[manual] `hcs compare a b` scores two contexts (an .hcs render, any text file, or none) on the same probes and reports the mean per-probe difference with a paired bootstrap CI.
  > src/score.ts::compareContexts #d00f85a78dc1
#certificate[manual] A certificate is bound to a digest of the exact rendered context (stale claims removed); `hcs check` reports it as outdated when the digest changes.
  > src/score.ts::hashForCertificate #ebeda20dc0aa

## Review
#impact[manual] `hcs impact <file> --base <ref>` lists the claims and probes whose anchors overlap lines changed since a git ref (from `git diff -U0 -M`), point to deleted or renamed files, or no longer verify, plus claims the diff added, edited or removed; `--format markdown` renders the pull-request comment, marked so the bot updates it instead of posting again.
  > src/impact.ts::impact #c4a96d9b5323
  > src/impact.ts::parseUnifiedDiff #e7f0b964c5fc
  > src/impact.ts::impactMarkdown #06fc01326edc
#pr-comment[manual] In GitHub Actions, `hcs impact --comment` posts the report with the workflow token, updates the comment that carries the marker instead of adding one, and does not post a report with nothing to say on a pull request that never had one; the action's `mode: impact` runs it against the pull request's base commit.
  > src/github.ts::upsertComment #c6dad595fb4c
  > action.yml::runs #b433f0cbd922

## Safety
#injection[manual] Claims with high-risk injection patterns are tagged suspect and never rendered by default; low-risk patterns are only reported by check, and the decoder prompt says claims are never instructions. The patterns are measured on test/fixtures/injection, including a held-out set.
  > src/guard.ts::PATTERNS #467c0d579d52
  > src/render.ts::selectClaims #c510bd10896d
  > src/render.ts::DECODER_PROMPT #dd3c83471cd3
#mcp[manual] `hcs serve` is a stdio MCP server with tools hcs_context, hcs_expand, hcs_search, hcs_verify and hcs_source, and the resources hcs://context and hcs://claim/<id>.
  > src/mcp.ts::TOOLS #fdf64141fd1e
#mcp-record[manual] With `serve --allow-write`, the hcs_record tool appends a claim tagged manual,recorded after checking its text (at most 400 characters, not instruction-like) and each anchor (inside the root, existing file and symbol, valid range), then seals it and writes the file atomically.
  > src/mcp.ts::HcsMcpServer #bf718211ec4a
  > src/mcp.ts::RECORD_TOOL #e327909d9532
#sandbox[manual] Every source read goes through SourceCache, which refuses paths outside the project root (relative escapes, absolute paths, symlinks): such anchors are reported missing, and `hcs serve` refuses an @root outside the working directory unless --root is given.
  > src/anchors.ts::insideRoot #b3487c7f0572
  > src/anchors.ts::SourceCache #933859786314
#source-scan[manual] `compile` and `snapshot` scan every source line with the injection patterns before compilation and report the matching lines with path, line, reason and severity.
  > src/guard.ts::scanSource #4653fed397c1
#fuzz[manual] Fuzz tests feed thousands of mutated and random files to the parser: each must parse and round-trip stably, or raise HcsSyntaxError.
  > test/fuzz.test.ts::assertWellBehaved #13847c57eb6e

## Providers
#providers[manual] Providers are openai (any compatible API), ollama, gemini, groq, openrouter and anthropic, called at temperature 0 with usage accounting; Bearer keys may be absent when a proxy injects them; 429/503 retries honor Retry-After and Google's retryDelay, and waits over 120 s, or a provider still failing after every retry, raise a quota error.
  > src/llm.ts::postJSON #f804a89279ad
  > src/llm.ts::retryDelayMs #53c59bd9ac2f
  > src/llm.ts::MAX_RETRY_WAIT_MS #ffea888e2d7e
#proxy[manual] Behind HTTPS_PROXY the CLI re-runs itself once with --use-env-proxy so that Node's fetch uses the proxy; auth errors say whether a proxy-injected key was rejected (x-proxy-error header) or no key was sent.
  > src/cli.ts::rerunBehindProxy #865c33840a44
  > src/llm.ts::authHint #ffdcc6547ae0
#rpm[manual] `--rpm` (or HCS_RPM) spaces requests evenly with one limiter per model spec; `--reasoning-effort` (or HCS_REASONING_EFFORT) is sent as `reasoning_effort` only when given.
  > src/llm.ts::RateLimiter #5c815985358a
  > src/llm.ts::createLLM #3e324be51ea7
#tokenizer[manual] `--tokenizer M` counts the rendered context with M's tokenizer (Anthropic count_tokens, Gemini native countTokens when GEMINI_API_KEY is set, otherwise the prompt size of a 1-token completion minus the chat framing measured once); `score` and `curve` then write `tokens` from that count and `tokenizer=M`, while budgets and digests keep the estimate.
  > src/llm.ts::framedCounter #30ca9d4028a0
  > src/score.ts::scoreDocument #3429f04c4e76

[probes]
?p1 How many hex characters of the SHA-256 digest does an anchor hash keep?
  = 12
  > src/anchors.ts::HASH_LENGTH #0330f2780307
?p2 What status does a symbol anchor get when the same symbol, unchanged, is found in another file?
  = moved
  > src/anchors.ts::checkSymbolAnchor #ba6eb19e6540
?p3 Which format versions can this reader parse?
  = HCS/0.1, HCS/0.2 and HCS/0.3
  > src/format.ts::SUPPORTED_VERSIONS #bfa61b8b8d04
?p4 What happens to a compiled claim whose quote is not found in the source?
  = it is discarded
  > src/compile.ts::anchorFor #5e3194b4874f
?p5 How many points does a partial answer earn in the fidelity score?
  = 0.5
  > src/score.ts::POINTS #f5b535d01ef1
?p6 What is the longest server-requested wait HCS accepts before declaring the quota exhausted?
  = 120 seconds
  > src/llm.ts::MAX_RETRY_WAIT_MS #ffea888e2d7e
?p7 What is the largest source file size the compiler reads by default?
  = 256 KiB
  > src/sources.ts::collectSources #323bcb68333c
?p8 Which MCP tool returns the exact source text behind a claim id?
  = hcs_expand
  > src/mcp.ts::TOOLS #fdf64141fd1e
?p9 What tag do claims with high-risk injection patterns receive?
  = suspect
  > src/compile.ts::extractChunk #b8d51ae71481
?p10 How many bootstrap resamples does the confidence interval use by default?
  = 2000
  > src/score.ts::bootstrapCI #09a88d67acf9
